# requirements/ — the spine

One file per requirement: `REQ-<id>.md` = YAML frontmatter (the JOINS) +
prose body (the SRS: Statement / Rationale / Acceptance). This directory's
PRESENCE activates `ultra trace`; deleting it returns the product to
dormancy. The full model is `references/trace.md` in the vendored skill.

The laws, short form:

- **No requirement ids in code, ever.** `operations[]` joins on the governed
  operationIds in the committed openapi.json — rename an operation and
  `ultra breaking` names it; update the join in the same landing.
- **Statuses are derived, never written.** You write draft|approved;
  `ultra trace` derives implemented / verified / validated from the contract
  and the recorded runs (`.ultra/test.json`, `.ultra/e2e.json`).
- **`ultra req approve` is the only path to approved** — it stamps who/when
  (the WP.22 validation record) and refuses while `- open:` questions stand.
- **Ask, don't guess**: `ultra req ask <id> "<question>"` parks the question;
  trace renders blocked-on-human; the answer is written back as a dated Q&A.
- **Changes are logged, rejections included**: `ultra req change` appends the
  WP.03 entry; an ACCEPTED change drops the requirement back to draft.
- **Requirements are the sharding unit**: one agent, one REQ file, disjoint
  territories — derived records cannot merge-conflict.

`EXAMPLE.md` shows the shape (trace ignores it — only `REQ-*.md` files
count). `exempt.txt` lists infrastructure operations no requirement claims.
